/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Valid Parentheses
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Stack
Difficulty : Easy

Problem:
Given a string containing only '(', ')', '{', '}', '[' and ']',
determine whether the brackets are valid.

A valid string must satisfy:
- Every opening bracket has a matching closing bracket.
- Brackets are closed in the correct order.
- Every closing bracket has a corresponding opening bracket.

Approach:
Use a stack to keep track of opening brackets.

Whenever an opening bracket is encountered, push it onto the
stack. When a closing bracket is encountered, compare it with
the bracket at the top of the stack.

If they form a valid pair, remove the opening bracket from the
stack. Otherwise, the string is invalid.

Why does this work?

A stack follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle. The most
recently opened bracket must be the first one to be closed.

Therefore, the top of the stack always represents the opening
bracket that should match the current closing bracket.

Algorithm:
1. Create an empty stack.
2. Traverse the string character by character.
3. Push every opening bracket onto the stack.
4. For a closing bracket:
   - Check whether the stack is empty.
   - Compare it with the top opening bracket.
   - If they match, pop the opening bracket.
   - Otherwise, return false.
5. After processing the string, return true only if the stack
   is empty.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
Each character is processed exactly once.

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
In the worst case, all characters can be opening brackets and
stored in the stack.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string s) {

        // Stores opening brackets until their matching
        // closing brackets are encountered.
        stack<char> brackets;

        for (char currentBracket : s) {

            // Store opening brackets.
            if (currentBracket == '(' ||
                currentBracket == '{' ||
                currentBracket == '[') {

                brackets.push(currentBracket);
            }
            else {

                // A closing bracket without an opening
                // bracket is invalid.
                if (brackets.empty()) {
                    return false;
                }

                char topBracket = brackets.top();

                // Check whether the brackets form a valid pair.
                if ((currentBracket == ')' && topBracket == '(') ||
                    (currentBracket == '}' && topBracket == '{') ||
                    (currentBracket == ']' && topBracket == '[')) {

                    brackets.pop();
                }
                else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        // All brackets must have been matched.
        return brackets.empty();
    }
};
